<!-- Exercise 054 - DataTemplateSelector (intermediate).
     Goal:   Render a mixed-type collection through Avalonia's implicit, type-keyed
     DataTemplates - there is no WPF-style DataTemplateSelector, and none is
     needed: an ItemsControl picks a template out of its DataTemplates collection
     by matching each item's runtime type against every template's DataType.
     Drills: ItemsControl.DataTemplates, DataTemplate DataType, implicit dispatch.
     Passes: dotnet test, filtered to Ex054_ (see
     Ex054_DataTemplateSelector.axaml.cs for the exact command - an XML comment
     cannot hold two hyphens in a row). -->
<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:i="clr-namespace:FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate"
             x:Class="FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Intermediate.Ex054_DataTemplateSelector"
             x:DataType="i:Ex054_DataTemplateSelectorViewModel">
  <!-- TODO: replace the placeholder with a single ItemsControl named "Pets", its
       ItemsSource bound to Pets, and a DataTemplates collection holding one
       DataTemplate per pet type, each rendering {CompiledBinding Label}:
         <ItemsControl Name="Pets" ItemsSource="{CompiledBinding Pets}">
           <ItemsControl.DataTemplates>
             <DataTemplate DataType="i:Ex054_Dog">
               <TextBlock Text="{CompiledBinding Label}" />
             </DataTemplate>
             <DataTemplate DataType="i:Ex054_Cat">
               <TextBlock Text="{CompiledBinding Label}" />
             </DataTemplate>
           </ItemsControl.DataTemplates>
         </ItemsControl>
       A fixed set of hard-coded TextBlocks reproduces the same three strings with
       no ItemsControl and no template at all - the test looks up a control
       specifically typed ItemsControl by name, and separately appends a FOURTH
       pet (another dog, breaking the dog/cat/dog alternation) after the initial
       render and checks a new TextBlock appears with the right text - something
       a hard-coded panel can never do. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Text="TODO" />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
